(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What patient education does the deck give for actinic keratosis?","choices":["Avoid all sun exposure permanently rather than using broad-spectrum protection or protective clothing; stop field therapy at the first sign of erythema or crusting; attend annual review regardless of any change in a lesion","No specific education is needed once the lesions have been treated successfully, since the surrounding field carries no further risk and reactions to therapy are not expected","Daily broad-spectrum sun protection and protective clothing; expect local treatment reactions such as erythema and crusting and adhere to the field therapy duration; seek prompt review of any non-healing or changing lesion","Sun protection in the summer months only and no protective clothing; expect no local reaction to field treatment; return only if an entirely new lesion appears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — warning about the expected reaction protects adherence. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which initial skin-directed therapies does the deck list for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids, topical mechlorethamine, bexarotene gel, and ultraviolet phototherapy","Topical corticosteroids, topical fluorouracil, imiquimod, and photodynamic therapy","Intralesional vincristine, vinblastine, bleomycin, and radiation","Liposomal doxorubicin, paclitaxel, romidepsin and vorinostat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — four skin-directed options. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which two clinical clues does the deck give for suspecting cutaneous T-cell lymphoma in a chronic eruption?","choices":["Itch out of proportion to the apparent inflammatory activity, and follicular involvement with hair loss","Itch that is entirely absent even in extensive disease, and complete sparing of the hair follicles","Sharp demarcation with thick silvery scale, and rapid complete response to a topical corticosteroid","Annular plaques with progressive central clearing, and a positive potassium hydroxide preparation from the border"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — folliculotropism discriminates from routine eczema or psoriasis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How common is basal cell carcinoma, and which ultraviolet exposure pattern drives it?","choices":["It is the fourth most common cancer overall, driven by intense intermittent ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people","It is a rare cancer, driven by human herpesvirus 8 in the setting of immune senescence","It is the most common form of cancer, driven by intense, INTERMITTENT ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people","It is the second most common skin cancer, driven by prolonged CUMULATIVE ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — intermittent, unlike the cumulative pattern of squamous cell carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What warning does the deck give about oedema in Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Marked oedema may occur even with few or no visible skin lesions, so oedema severity must not be used to gauge disease burden","Marked oedema occurs only in advanced disease with many visible lesions, so its severity reliably indicates the cutaneous disease burden","Marked oedema is never a feature of Kaposi sarcoma at any stage, and its presence should prompt a search for venous thrombosis instead","Marked oedema occurs only in the classic form of the disease, and its severity can therefore be used to distinguish classic from epidemic Kaposi sarcoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the skin can look almost clear while disease is extensive. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which clinical forms of Kaposi sarcoma does the deck describe?","choices":["Patch, plaque, tumour and erythrodermic disease, each occurring in a different population and each representing a successive stage of the same underlying process","Classic in older men with a chronic course; endemic, often aggressive in young Black men in equatorial Africa; iatrogenic with immunosuppressive therapy; and epidemic in acquired immunodeficiency","Superficial, nodular, pigmented and morpheaform disease, each occurring in a different population and each with a different histologic behaviour and course","Superficial spreading, lentigo maligna, nodular and acral lentiginous disease, each occurring in a different population and each with a different growth pattern and course"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — four forms, each with a different population and course. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"How is squamous cell carcinoma in situ managed when there are no high-risk features?","choices":["Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery in every case, whatever the features and whatever the site","Programmed death 1 blockade or cetuximab in every case, whatever the features and whatever the site","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy alone, with no role for topical therapy, curettage or electrodesiccation","Imiquimod, topical fluorouracil, or curettage and electrodesiccation in appropriately selected disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — three options for in situ disease without high-risk features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which systemic agents are first-line in Kaposi sarcoma, and what does the deck say about combining them with antiretroviral therapy?","choices":["Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel; antiretroviral therapy plus chemotherapy is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone in advanced disease","Liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel; antiretroviral therapy alone is more effective than the combination in advanced disease","Vismodegib and sonidegib; antiretroviral therapy plus a hedgehog inhibitor is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone","Romidepsin and vorinostat; antiretroviral therapy plus a histone deacetylase inhibitor is more effective than antiretroviral therapy alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — combination beats antiretroviral therapy alone once disease is advanced. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which chemoprevention does the deck give for squamous cell carcinoma, at what dose, and with what effect?","choices":["Nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily, reducing new squamous cell carcinoma development by approximately 20% in high-risk patients","Oral acitretin 25 mg daily, reducing new squamous cell carcinoma development by approximately 30% in high-risk patients","Topical fluorouracil twice daily, reducing new squamous cell carcinoma development by approximately 50% in high-risk patients","Nicotinamide 500 mg orally twice daily, reducing new squamous cell carcinoma development by approximately 30% in high-risk patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the corresponding figure for basal cell carcinoma is about 20%. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which warning patterns does the deck give for basal cell carcinoma, and where do they commonly appear?","choices":["A pearly papule, an erythematous patch larger than 6 mm, or a non-healing ulcer — commonly on the palms, soles and nail units","A violaceous macule, a purple plaque, or an oral lesion — commonly on the hard palate and lower limbs","A pearly papule, an erythematous patch larger than 6 mm, or a non-healing ulcer — commonly on the face, trunk or lower legs","A sandpaper-textured papule, a pigmented macule larger than 6 mm, or a warty nodule — commonly on the scalp, ears and dorsal hands"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — three patterns, three common sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which situations does the deck give as referral indications in actinic keratosis?","choices":["Any lesion on the dorsal hand or forearm; any patient over the age of 50; any lesion larger than 3 mm; any lesion that has been present more than a year","Only after biopsy has already confirmed invasive squamous cell carcinoma, since uncertainty, lesion burden, recurrence and immunosuppression are all managed in primary care","Only when the patient requests it for cosmetic reasons, since diagnostic uncertainty, high lesion burden and recurrence are all managed without referral","Diagnostic uncertainty or high lesion burden; recurrent or persistent lesions after appropriate therapy; immunosuppression, cosmetically sensitive sites, or concern for squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — three groupings. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the Hutchinson sign in the nail unit, and what should it prompt?","choices":["Pigment confined strictly beneath the nail plate without any periungual extension — reassuring, and allowing routine follow-up whatever the other features","Vesicles on the tip or side of the nose — concerning for ocular involvement in herpes zoster, prompting same-day ophthalmology review","A small red-blue subungual focus with paroxysmal pain and cold sensitivity — concerning for a glomus tumour, prompting imaging and specialist review","Periungual pigment extending onto the proximal nail fold — highly concerning for nail unit melanoma, prompting urgent expert evaluation regardless of other features"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it outranks the rest of the picture. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What treatment philosophy does the deck set out for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Total-skin electron-beam treatment as the initial therapy for every patient regardless of stage, because skin-directed topical therapy has no demonstrated benefit","A stage-directed, skin-first approach for most early disease, because early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure or prevent progression and may cause complications and premature death","Immediate aggressive systemic chemotherapy for all stages regardless of extent, because early aggressive treatment reliably cures the disease and prevents progression to systemic involvement","Observation alone at every stage without skin-directed treatment, because no therapy has been shown to alter the course of the disease or to relieve symptoms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — aggression is a harm here, not a virtue. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is the core mechanism of actinic keratosis, and how does the deck position it relative to keratinocyte carcinoma?","choices":["Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic melanocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant but a distinct entity quite separate from keratinocyte carcinoma, with no biologic continuum between them","Human papillomavirus infection of keratinocytes in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is a benign lesion lying on a continuum with verruca rather than with keratinocyte carcinoma","Chronic thermal injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of scarred skin; it is premalignant and lies on a continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma but is unrelated to ultraviolet exposure","Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma, not a distinct separate entity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a continuum, not a separate disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which red flags does the deck list for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Sandpaper texture, tenderness on palpation, flesh-coloured hue, and a size under 6 mm","Rapid growth, pain, bleeding, ulceration, induration, fixation, or palpable regional nodes","Slow growth, painlessness, a stable size, soft consistency, free mobility, and no palpable nodes","Pruritus, fine scaling, symmetry of the lesion, sharp demarcation, and a waxy stuck-on surface"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — seven features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which features are NOT part of a typical actinic keratosis and should prompt biopsy consideration?","choices":["Bleeding, induration, ulceration or rapid enlargement","Tenderness on palpation and a rough surface texture","Flesh-coloured or pink colour and a size under a centimetre","Location on the dorsal hand and the presence of surrounding sun damage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — none of these belong to a typical lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How should a suspected basal cell carcinoma be sampled?","choices":["With a wide local excision carrying 1 cm margins","With a potassium hydroxide preparation of the surface scale","With a sentinel lymph node biopsy at the time of diagnosis","With a shave or punch biopsy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the histologic subtype then dictates treatment. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does the deck say should be used to distinguish squamous cell carcinoma from its differential, rather than morphology alone?","choices":["Time course, firmness or induration, ulceration, site, immune status and pathology","Colour variegation, symmetry, border regularity, diameter and evolution alone","Patient age, sex, family history and occupational sun exposure alone","Dermoscopy pattern, Wood lamp fluorescence and surface scale alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — six discriminators beyond appearance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where does nail unit melanoma most often arise, which sites are high-yield, and is it ultraviolet-driven?","choices":["Most often in the hyponychium; the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites; it is driven by high-risk human papillomavirus","Most often in the matrix; the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites; it is NOT clearly ultraviolet-driven and may occur in any skin tone","Most often in the nail bed; the index finger and little toe are high-yield sites; it is clearly ultraviolet-driven and occurs mainly in fair skin","Most often in the proximal nail fold; all digits equally; it is clearly ultraviolet-driven and occurs mainly in fair skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — delayed recognition contributes to advanced-stage presentation. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What do the letters of the melanoma recognition mnemonic stand for?","choices":["Age over 50 at onset; Border irregularity; Colour variegation; Depth greater than 1 mm; Evolution — change in the lesion","Asymmetry; Border irregular, notched or poorly defined; Colour variegation; Diameter greater than 6 cm; Evolution — change in the lesion","Asymmetry; Border irregular, notched or poorly defined; Colour variegation; Diameter greater than 6 mm; Evolution — change in the lesion","Asymmetry of the halves; Bleeding from the surface; Crusting over the lesion; Diameter greater than 6 mm; Erythema surrounding the lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — though smaller lesions can still be melanoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which re-excision margins does the deck give for definitive local treatment of melanoma?","choices":["In situ 0.5 to 1 cm; less than 1 mm thick, a 2 cm margin; more than 1 mm thick, a 0.5 cm margin","A uniform 1 cm margin for every melanoma regardless of thickness or in situ status","In situ 0.5 to 1 cm; less than 1 mm thick, a 1 cm margin; more than 1 mm thick, a 1 to 2 cm margin","In situ 2 cm; less than 1 mm thick, a 3 cm margin; more than 1 mm thick, a 5 cm margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — margin scales with Breslow thickness. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the overall prognosis of basal cell carcinoma, and where does its morbidity come from?","choices":["Usually rapidly growing and rarely curable even when treated early; morbidity comes chiefly from early distant metastasis to lung and liver, with local destruction uncommon","Usually slow-growing and highly curable when treated early; morbidity comes chiefly from nodal metastasis, which occurs in 3 to 7% of cases, rather than from local destruction","Usually slow-growing but uniformly fatal without systemic therapy; morbidity comes from delayed hedgehog inhibitor treatment rather than from local destruction or recurrence","Usually slow-growing and highly curable when treated early; morbidity comes from local destruction, recurrence, delayed diagnosis and anatomically complex sites, with metastatic disease rare but serious"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the danger is local destruction rather than spread. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the referral threshold for melanoma to an expert centre?","choices":["Only melanoma that has already failed immunotherapy","Melanoma deeper than 1 mm, or with lymph-node or other-site spread","Melanoma deeper than 4 mm only, since thinner disease is managed entirely in primary care","Any melanoma in a patient over 65, regardless of thickness or spread"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — confirmed melanoma warrants coordinated dermatology and surgical oncology care. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How is a typical actinic keratosis diagnosed?","choices":["Usually clinically, with dermoscopy supporting recognition when the clinician is trained","By shave or punch biopsy in every case, since clinical diagnosis is unreliable","By potassium hydroxide microscopy of the surface scale","By serology for ultraviolet-induced antibodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — biopsy is reserved for concerning or persistent lesions. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the classic presentation of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Red or purple macules, plaques and nodules on skin or mucous membranes; also marked edema with few visible lesions","A small red, conical, hard nodule that may ulcerate; also a non-healing ulcer, a warty nodule, or an irregular pink plaque with haemorrhagic crust","A pearly translucent papule with visible telangiectasias and a rolled border; also a scar-like ivory-white plaque with subtle extension","An asymmetric pigmented macule with irregular borders and colour variegation; also an amelanotic nodule lacking classic features"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — four presentations to recognise. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is mycosis fungoides, and how does it behave over time?","choices":["A vascular tumour caused by human herpesvirus 8 that begins in the skin and spreads to the viscera within months of the first cutaneous lesion appearing","A keratinocyte carcinoma that begins as an actinic keratosis in sun-damaged skin and becomes invasive over years before any potential nodal spread","A cutaneous T-cell lymphoma that begins in skin and may remain confined there for years or decades, with malignant T cells forming skin infiltrates before any potential systemic spread","A cutaneous B-cell lymphoma that begins in the bone marrow and seeds the skin within weeks, with malignant B cells forming infiltrates only after systemic spread"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — skin-limited for a long time in many patients. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Where do actinic keratoses occur, and what does finding several in one anatomic zone imply?","choices":["Sun-exposed face, scalp, ears, forearms and dorsal hands; multiple lesions in one zone suggest field cancerization and favour field-directed therapy","Sun-protected trunk, axillae and groin; multiple lesions in one zone suggest an immunosuppressed host and favour systemic therapy","Palms, soles and nail beds; multiple lesions in one zone suggest acral disease and favour surgical excision","Sun-exposed face, scalp and forearms; multiple lesions in one zone suggest a lower risk and favour observation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the distribution drives the treatment choice. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which history elements does the deck ask you to elicit?","choices":["Onset and change; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars and radiation sites; family history — but not immunosuppression or transplant status, which do not alter cutaneous malignancy risk","Onset and change, pain or pruritus; occupational and recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; immunosuppression and family history — but not prior skin cancer, which does not predict a second primary","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational or recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; prior skin cancer, immunosuppression or transplant, human immunodeficiency virus risk; chronic scars, wounds or radiation sites; family history","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars, wounds and radiation sites; family history — but not prior skin cancer, immunosuppression, transplant or human immunodeficiency virus status, which the dermatologist obtains at the time of biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — note chronic scars and radiation sites among them. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What self-examination does the deck ask melanoma patients to perform, and which sites must be included?","choices":["No self-examination at all, since surveillance of the skin, scalp, nails and acral sites is entirely the specialist team's responsibility","Monthly self-examination using the recognition mnemonic and ugly-duckling principles, including the scalp, back, palms, soles and nails","Annual self-examination using the recognition mnemonic alone, limited to the sun-exposed skin of the face, arms and upper chest","Monthly self-examination of the trunk and limbs only, since acral, nail and scalp melanoma are too rare to warrant routine checking"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the acral and scalp sites are easy to miss. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which findings make referral URGENT in squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Only failure of two courses of topical therapy, whatever the site, size, histology, immune status or symptoms","High-risk site, size, recurrence, aggressive histology, immunosuppression, neurologic symptoms, or nodal disease","Cosmetic concern, patient anxiety, a lesion older than one year, a lesion on the trunk, or any pigmented lesion","Only confirmed nodal disease, since high-risk site, size, recurrence and immune status can all be managed routinely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — neurologic symptoms suggest perineural invasion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
